WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:03.899
Let's get into audience questions for the
week and some really good ones.

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:07.380
First one is about these two special elections
in California and Wisconsin.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.670
The question is, hey, David, do California
and Wisconsin special elections going to Republicans

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:19.410
mean an inevitable disaster for Democrats
in November of 2020?

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:20.450
The answer is no.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:21.450
The answer is no.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.300
So if you've not been following these two
races, you had the 7th Congressional District

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.770
in Wisconsin and the 25th Congressional District
in California.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.279
Two special elections.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:32.829
Both went to the Republican candidates.

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:40.589
Mike Garcia defeating Christy Smith in California's
25th Wisconsin 7th, went to the Republican

00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:42.140
candidate, Tom Tiffany.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.699
So let let's talk first about California and
then about Wisconsin.

00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:51.050
But before we even do that, special elections
tend to have low turnout.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.260
And in particular, we know that one party
benefits from lower turnout.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.829
Republicans benefit from lower turnout.

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:03.089
So you have to be very careful about taking
especially not just any special election,

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:07.980
but in the case of California's 25th, a special
election in a historically red district, at

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.790
least when it comes to members of Congress
and saying, hey, alert, low turnout, special

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:13.930
election went to the Republican.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.200
This spells trouble in the fall.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.200
It could.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.009
There could be trouble in the fall for Democrats
and for Joe Biden in the 25th.

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:21.400
But this is not evidence of it.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.670
So let's start with California, 25th, California
25th is this district that was held by Democratic

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.549
Congresswoman Katie Hill.

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:30.689
Katie Hill ended up resigning.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:35.609
She resigned because she was involved in a
relationship with a staffer there.

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:39.790
Nothing illegal took place, but it does violate
House ethics guidelines.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.659
And so she resigned.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:44.640
So a couple of things then went on.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:53.640
You had this jungle primary for the November
race simultaneously with the special election

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.710
primary.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:01.270
You with the special election, you had a blowback
effect where this is, you know, they call

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.659
it a purple district.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:09.990
But this was a seat that was held for 25 years
by Republican Buck McKeon and then for four

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:11.910
years by Republican Steve Knight.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.701
They call it a poor purple district, but it's
been a Republican held district at the congressional

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:19.960
level for a really, really long time.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.060
And then it went to Katie Hill, and Katie
Hill had to resign.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.980
And there was a blowback effect to some degree
that has nothing to do with what we would

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.920
expect to see in the fall.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.470
So that's number one.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.820
Republicans are seeing the headline as, hey,
a Republican won a seat that was just held

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.900
by a Democrat.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.780
This is great for Republicans.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.250
The real story is that Katie Hill had taken
the seat that had been held by Republicans

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.710
for a really long time.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:49.670
And it doesn't tell us that Trump is going
to start winning blue states in 2020.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.500
Then you get to Wisconsin's 7th.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.510
I actually think that Wisconsin 7th, although
it got less attention than the California

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.530
25th, is more relevant for thinking about
2020.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:07.110
If you look at the results of the Wisconsin
7th special election just recently and compare

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:12.840
it to how those areas voted in 2016, there
was really no major shift.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:18.100
If you look at the elements that make up the
Wisconsin seventh and you look at how did

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:23.540
they vote on Trump versus Hillary in 2016
and how did they vote for Tiffany versus the

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:29.330
Democratic challenger in that recent special
election that you could say, well, maybe I

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.180
believe it's pronounced a naida or Anita.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.230
Anita County.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:40.640
That might be a flip that came within 20 votes
in the judicial election that just took place.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.540
It's going to be pretty similar to 2016.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:50.880
So Wisconsin 7th is potentially more consequential
when thinking about 2020 because any small

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.100
change in Wisconsin could flip the state.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:01.560
When you look at California, you can argue
about California's 25th as a sort of guidepost

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.660
for what might happen.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.890
Bigger picture in California.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.010
But Trump's not going to win California no
matter what.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:08.010
Right?

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.460
Katie Hill resigned.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:13.960
She was a Democrat, now a Republican, won
in a Republican state four decades before

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.030
it was Katie.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.170
It doesn't matter because Biden's going to
win California.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:24.030
In Wisconsin, even these small shifts, the
margin was small enough in Wisconsin.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.710
I don't remember exactly what I think was
about 20000 votes.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:33.080
That is a state that if the wind blows one
way versus the other, if older voters abandoned

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.860
Trump and go to by the way, we've been seeing
you could actually get a different result

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.560
where Donald Trump doesn't win Wisconsin.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.360
So I don't think either tells us what is in
store for November.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.410
But if I were to look at one of them as maybe
being a more impactful signal, it would be

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.099
looking at Wisconsin seventh, not California's
25th.

